Q1 – Processes and Process States
Problem context: An online examination server creates student sessions, auto-save work and proctoring tasks. With 2,000 students logging in together, excessive process creation, memory use, context switching and I/O waiting can reduce performance.
1.1 Process-state diagram
          admitted                 dispatch
+-----+ -------------> +-------+ -------------> +---------+
| NEW |                | READY |                | RUNNING |
+-----+                +-------+ <------------- +---------+
                           ^       pre-emption       |   |
                           |                         |   | exit
              I/O complete|                         |   v
                      +---------+ <--- I/O wait ---+---------+
                      | WAITING |                  |TERMINATED|
                      +---------+                  +---------+

A process is NEW when it is being created. READY means it is in memory and waiting for CPU time. RUNNING means its instructions are executing. WAITING (blocked) means it is waiting for an event such as disk, network or keyboard I/O. TERMINATED means execution has ended.
Transition	Meaning
New → Ready	The operating system admits the process.
Ready → Running	The CPU scheduler dispatches the process.
Running → Ready	The time slice expires or a higher-priority process pre-empts it.
Running → Waiting	The process requests I/O or waits for an event.
Waiting → Ready	The required I/O/event completes.
Running → Terminated	The process exits or is stopped.

1.2 Process Control Block (PCB)
+----------------------------------+
|       PROCESS CONTROL BLOCK      |
+----------------------------------+
| Process ID (PID)                 |
| Process state                    |
| Program counter                  |
| CPU registers / stack pointer    |
| CPU scheduling information       |
| Memory-management information    |
| Accounting information           |
| I/O status and open-file details |
| Parent/child and signal details  |
+----------------------------------+

The PCB is the kernel data structure that stores the information needed to manage and resume a process. During a context switch, the OS saves the running process's CPU state in its PCB and restores the next process's state.
1.3 C program using fork(), ps and /proc
The following Linux/POSIX program creates a child. The parent and child print their PIDs. A short sleep keeps the child visible long enough to inspect it.
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
 
int main(void) {
    pid_t pid = fork();
 
    if (pid < 0) {
        perror("fork");
        return 1;
    }
    if (pid == 0) {
        printf("Child: PID=%ld, PPID=%ld\n",
               (long)getpid(), (long)getppid());
        sleep(15);
        printf("Child exiting\n");
        _exit(0);
    }
 
    printf("Parent: PID=%ld, child PID=%ld\n",
           (long)getpid(), (long)pid);
    printf("Inspect the child using: ps -o pid,ppid,stat,cmd -p %ld\n",
           (long)pid);
    waitpid(pid, NULL, 0);
    printf("Parent: child completed\n");
    return 0;
}

