1.4 Performance evaluation for 2,000 sessions
Factor	Effect at high process count	Evaluation
Context switching	CPU time is spent saving/restoring registers, memory mappings and scheduler state.	Frequent switching reduces useful application work.
Memory	Each process has its own virtual address space, page tables and kernel bookkeeping.	2,000 independent address spaces can consume substantially more memory.
I/O waiting	Network, disk and video operations can leave many processes blocked.	Blocked processes do not use CPU, but still occupy resources.
Scheduling	A large runnable queue increases scheduling and cache pressure.	Latency can rise, especially when jobs are short.

1.5 Design decision
Design	Advantages	Disadvantages
One process per student	Strong isolation; one crash normally does not crash other sessions.	Higher memory and process-management overhead; IPC is needed.
One thread per student	Lower creation and switching overhead; shared memory makes communication easy.	A faulty thread can damage the whole server; shared data needs locks.
Fixed worker pool	Limits concurrency; predictable resource use; queue absorbs bursts.	A queue can build up if pool is too small; needs load balancing.

Recommendation: Use a fixed-size worker-thread pool for ordinary request processing, with separate processes only for risky or strongly isolated components such as video decoding. A bounded request queue prevents unlimited worker creation. This balances memory, throughput and fault isolation better than creating one full process for every student.
1.6 Alternative: one thread per student
Threads in the same process share code, global data, heap and open file descriptors. Each thread has its own stack, registers, program counter and thread-local storage. A thread generally needs much less memory than a separate process, although stack size depends on the platform and configuration. Thread switches can be cheaper because the address space is shared, but they still require scheduler and register-state work.
A thread crash caused by an invalid memory write can terminate or corrupt the entire server process. A process-per-session design offers stronger isolation. Therefore, a bounded thread pool is preferred for normal sessions, while process isolation should be used where a crash must not affect the main service.
1.7 Complexity and conclusion
Creating and tracking N independent processes requires O(N) process records and at least O(N) total per-process bookkeeping; a thread pool with K workers uses O(K) worker stacks plus O(N) queued request records. Processing each request still depends on the request workload. Since K is bounded and normally much smaller than N, the pool controls memory and scheduling overhead. The server should use admission control, asynchronous I/O and monitoring to prevent overload.
