2.8 Multilevel Feedback Queue (MLFQ) and recommendation
An MLFQ places new jobs in a high-priority queue with a short quantum. A job that repeatedly uses its entire quantum is moved to a lower-priority queue with a longer quantum. Interactive and short jobs therefore receive quick service, while CPU-intensive long jobs still make progress. Periodic priority boosts or ageing prevent starvation.
Recommendation: Use an MLFQ or a hybrid priority + Round Robin policy for the video service. It favours short thumbnail tasks and remains fair to long encoding jobs. The measured example shows SRTF has the lowest average waiting time, but real video burst lengths are not always known in advance. MLFQ is more practical when job length is uncertain.
Complexity: A simple ready-queue insertion/removal can be O(1) with linked queues. SJF/SRTF selection can be O(n) by scanning n jobs, or O(log n) with a priority heap. MLFQ scheduling cost is small per queue operation, while its policy and tuning add complexity.
