3.5 Race condition and protection
If two threads execute counter++ at the same time, both may read the same old value and write back the same incremented value. One update is lost. This is a race condition. Protect the update with a pthread mutex, or use a C11 atomic counter when only an atomic increment is required.
pthread_mutex_lock(&counter_mutex);
counter++;
pthread_mutex_unlock(&counter_mutex);
 
/* Alternative for a simple counter: */
atomic_fetch_add(&atomic_counter, 1);

3.6 Alternative: multiple processes
Separate processes have independent address spaces, so a memory fault in one camera worker is less likely to corrupt another. However, process creation, context switching and memory use are generally higher. Results must be combined through pipes, sockets, message queues or shared memory. Threads are preferred when workers share large frame buffers; processes are useful when decoding libraries are unstable or strong fault isolation is required.
For N frames, the useful processing work is O(N). A pool of K workers ideally reduces parallel processing time toward O(N/K), but actual speed-up is bounded by serial work, available CPU cores, memory bandwidth and I/O. A fixed pool of approximately the number of effective CPU cores is a good starting point.
