The parent must close fd[1] after writing. When every open write end is closed and the pipe buffer becomes empty, the child's read() returns 0 (EOF). If the parent forgets to close fd[1], the kernel still sees a writer open, so the child waits for more data and does not finish.
4.3 Shared-memory alternative
Shared memory maps the same memory region into both processes. It avoids copying every reading through a pipe after the shared region is established, but it requires explicit coordination. A producer/consumer design can use a POSIX shared-memory object plus two semaphores: one counting available slots and one counting available readings. A mutex protects the shared indices and buffer.
Conceptual shared-memory producer/consumer:
1. shm_open() and ftruncate() create a shared object.
2. mmap() maps it into both processes.
3. Initialise shared buffer, read/write indices and semaphores.
4. Reader: wait(empty), lock, write item, unlock, post(full).
5. Analyser: wait(full), lock, read item, unlock, post(empty).
6. Send an explicit end-of-stream marker, then unmap and unlink.

