Q5 – Synchronisation: Critical Sections, Semaphores and Monitors
In railway ticket booking, several agents may try to reserve the same seat. The shared seat state must be updated atomically to prevent duplicate sales.
5.1 Critical-section requirements
Requirement	Meaning in ticket booking
Mutual exclusion	Only one agent at a time updates the same seat record.
Progress	If the critical section is free and agents want to enter, selection of the next agent cannot be postponed indefinitely.
Bounded waiting	An agent that requests entry must not be bypassed forever; a bound exists on the number of other entries before it gets a turn.

5.2 Peterson’s solution
For two processes P0 and P1, Peterson’s algorithm uses flag[2] to show interest and turn to resolve simultaneous requests.
Shared: bool flag[2] = {false,false}; int turn;
Process i (j = 1-i):
    flag[i] = true;
    turn = j;
    while (flag[j] && turn == j) { }   // wait
    critical_section();
    flag[i] = false;
    remainder_section();

Mutual exclusion is maintained because when both processes want to enter, turn gives one priority. Progress is maintained because a process that is not interested cannot block the other. Bounded waiting is maintained because the turn variable prevents one process from repeatedly bypassing the other. On modern CPUs, compiler and hardware reordering can violate the assumptions of the textbook code unless suitable atomic operations and memory-order guarantees are used. It is mainly a teaching algorithm, not a replacement for OS mutexes.
5.3 Binary and counting semaphores
A binary semaphore has values 0 or 1 and can protect one critical section. A counting semaphore represents multiple identical available resources. wait (P) decrements the semaphore or blocks when no resource is available; signal (V) increments it and wakes a waiting thread if needed.
sem_wait(&seat_lock);        // enter protected region
if (seat_is_available(seat)) {
    mark_seat_booked(seat);
}
sem_post(&seat_lock);        // leave protected region

A single global binary semaphore is safe but can become a bottleneck. A lock per train or coach allows independent bookings to proceed concurrently, provided seat data is protected at the correct granularity.
5.4 Classic synchronisation problems
Bounded buffer (producer–consumer)
Use empty=buffer capacity, full=0 and a mutex. A producer waits(empty), locks, inserts an item, unlocks and signals(full). A consumer waits(full), locks, removes an item, unlocks and signals(empty).
Readers–writers
A reader count is protected by a mutex. The first reader locks the shared data resource; the last reader unlocks it. Writers lock the shared resource exclusively. A reader-preference version can starve writers, so a fair read-write lock or turnstile is preferred when bounded waiting is required.
Dining philosophers
Each philosopher needs two forks. Taking one fork and waiting indefinitely for the other can create circular wait. A common semaphore solution allows at most four of five philosophers to compete at once, or uses a waiter that grants both forks together. Resource ordering (always acquire the lower-numbered fork first) also prevents circular wait.
5.5 Monitor and condition variables
A monitor encapsulates shared data and operations so only one thread executes a monitor procedure at a time. Condition variables allow a thread to wait until a condition becomes true. For a ticket system, a bookSeat() monitor operation can check availability and mark the seat booked atomically. This reduces the chance of forgetting a lock or releasing it in the wrong place.
5.6 Completed semaphore bounded-buffer program
This version supports four producers and four consumers while ensuring exactly 20 distinct items are produced and consumed once. It uses a shared next-item counter protected by the mutex, plus empty/full semaphores.
#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
 
#define SIZE 5
#define ITEMS 20
#define PRODUCERS 4
#define CONSUMERS 4
 
int buffer[SIZE], in=0, out=0, next_item=1;
sem_t empty, full;
pthread_mutex_t lock=PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
long consumed_sum=0;
int consumed_count=0;
 
void *producer(void *arg) {
    (void)arg;
    for (;;) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        int item=next_item++;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        if (item>ITEMS) break;
 
        sem_wait(&empty);
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        buffer[in]=item;
        in=(in+1)%SIZE;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        sem_post(&full);
    }
    return NULL;
}
void *consumer(void *arg) {
    (void)arg;
    for (;;) {
        sem_wait(&full);
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        int item=buffer[out];
        out=(out+1)%SIZE;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        sem_post(&empty);
 
        if (item==-1) break;
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        consumed_sum+=item;
        consumed_count++;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}
int main(void) {
    pthread_t p[PRODUCERS], c[CONSUMERS];
    sem_init(&empty,0,SIZE); sem_init(&full,0,0);
    for(int i=0;i<CONSUMERS;i++)
        pthread_create(&c[i],NULL,consumer,NULL);
    for(int i=0;i<PRODUCERS;i++)
        pthread_create(&p[i],NULL,producer,NULL);
    for(int i=0;i<PRODUCERS;i++) pthread_join(p[i],NULL);
 
    /* One stop marker for each consumer. */
    for(int i=0;i<CONSUMERS;i++) {
        sem_wait(&empty);
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        buffer[in]=-1; in=(in+1)%SIZE;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        sem_post(&full);
    }
    for(int i=0;i<CONSUMERS;i++) pthread_join(c[i],NULL);
 
    printf("items consumed: %d, sum = %ld (expected %d)\n",
           consumed_count,consumed_sum,ITEMS*(ITEMS+1)/2);
    sem_destroy(&empty); sem_destroy(&full);
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&lock);
    return 0;
}

