5.7 What happens without the mutex?
Without the mutex, two producers may write to the same buffer position or update the circular index concurrently. Two consumers may read the same slot or corrupt the output index. Updates to consumed_sum and consumed_count may also be lost. The semaphores control the number of full and empty slots, but they do not automatically make multiple shared-variable operations atomic.
5.8 Lock granularity and deployment decision
Lock strategy	Advantages	Disadvantages
One global lock	Simple; easy to reason about.	All bookings serialize; poor throughput.
One lock per train	Different trains can be booked concurrently.	Bookings on the same train still serialize.
One lock per coach/seat group	High concurrency for unrelated seats.	More locks; lock ordering and multi-seat booking become harder.

Recommendation: Use one lock per train as the safe starting point, then measure contention. If each coach has independent seat inventory and reservations rarely span coaches, use per-coach locks. Multi-seat or cross-coach bookings must acquire locks in a fixed order to prevent deadlock. No throughput figures are supplied; benchmark all three strategies with the same workload before claiming a measured winner.
5.9 Atomic operations and spin locks
C11 atomic_fetch_add can update a simple counter without a mutex and is appropriate when the operation is a single atomic update. A spin lock repeatedly checks a lock variable; it may be efficient for extremely short critical sections on multiprocessor systems, but wastes CPU while waiting. A blocking mutex/semaphore is usually better for long critical sections or oversubscribed workloads.
Complexity: A protected counter update is O(1) per operation. A spin lock may use O(k) repeated checks while waiting k iterations. A mutex can block a waiting thread rather than consume CPU continuously. At 1, 4 and 16 threads, record throughput and CPU utilisation on the actual system; contention usually increases as thread count rises.
Q6 – Deadlock Handling and Banker’s Algorithm
The research centre shares GPUs, simulation licences and high-speed storage among jobs. A deadlock occurs when a set of processes is permanently blocked because each waits for a resource held by another.
6A.1 Four necessary conditions
Condition	Meaning in the cluster	Example
Mutual exclusion	At least one resource is non-shareable.	A GPU allocation is assigned exclusively to one job.
Hold and wait	A job holds one resource while requesting another.	A job holds a GPU and waits for a licence.
No pre-emption	Resources cannot be forcibly taken away safely.	A running simulation cannot release its GPU immediately.
Circular wait	A circular chain of jobs waits for resources held by the next job.	J1 waits for J2, J2 waits for J3, J3 waits for J1.

6A.2 Resource-allocation graph
     requests                         allocated
 J1 ---------> GPU <--------------------- J2
 ^                                      |
 |                                      | requests
 |                                      v
 |                                    LICENCE
 |                                      |
 |                                      v allocated
STORAGE <----------------------------- J3
  ^                                      |
  |                                      | requests
  +--------------------------------------+

In a resource-allocation graph, process/job nodes are circles and resource-type nodes are rectangles. A request edge points from a process to a resource; an assignment edge points from a resource instance to the process holding it. The cycle above illustrates circular wait. A cycle is necessary for deadlock in a single-instance resource system; with multiple instances, a cycle alone may not prove deadlock.
6A.3 Completed Banker’s algorithm program
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#define P 5
#define R 3
 
int avail[R]={3,3,2};
int maxm[P][R]={{7,5,3},{3,2,2},{9,0,2},{2,2,2},{4,3,3}};
int alloc[P][R]={{0,1,0},{2,0,0},{3,0,2},{2,1,1},{0,0,2}};
 
int is_safe(int seq[P]) {
    int work[R], finish[P]={0}, count=0;
    memcpy(work,avail,sizeof work);
    while(count<P) {
        int found=0;
        for(int p=0;p<P;p++) {
            if(finish[p]) continue;
            int possible=1;
            for(int r=0;r<R;r++)
                if(maxm[p][r]-alloc[p][r] > work[r]) possible=0;
            if(possible) {
                for(int r=0;r<R;r++) work[r]+=alloc[p][r];
                finish[p]=1;
                seq[count++]=p;
                found=1;
            }
        }
        if(!found) return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}
void request(int p,const int req[R]) {
    for(int r=0;r<R;r++)
        if(req[r]>maxm[p][r]-alloc[p][r] || req[r]>avail[r]) {
            printf("P%d request: must wait (not available)\n",p);
            return;
        }
    for(int r=0;r<R;r++){avail[r]-=req[r];alloc[p][r]+=req[r];}
    int seq[P];
    if(is_safe(seq)) {
        printf("P%d request: granted, safe sequence",p);
        for(int i=0;i<P;i++) printf(" P%d",seq[i]);
        printf("\n");
    } else {
        for(int r=0;r<R;r++){avail[r]+=req[r];alloc[p][r]-=req[r];}
        printf("P%d request: denied (unsafe)\n",p);
    }
}
int main(void) {
    int seq[P];
    if(is_safe(seq)) {
        printf("initial state safe:");
        for(int i=0;i<P;i++) printf(" P%d",seq[i]);
        printf("\n");
    } else printf("initial state unsafe\n");
    request(1,(int[R]){1,0,2});
    request(4,(int[R]){3,3,0});
    request(0,(int[R]){0,2,0});
    return 0;
}

