Available = (3,3,2). P1 can finish because its need (1,2,2) is no greater than available; after it finishes, its allocation is released. Repeating the test gives the safe order P1 → P3 → P4 → P0 → P2. A safe sequence means every process can finish in that order if it requests no more than its declared maximum.
6A.5 Why P0 request (0,2,0) is unsafe
After P1's request is granted, the available vector becomes (2,3,0). P4's request (3,3,0) cannot be granted because only 2 units of resource A are available. Next P0 requests (0,2,0), leaving Available = (2,1,0). The new P0 allocation becomes (0,3,0), so its remaining Need is (7,2,3).
With Available (2,1,0), P0 cannot finish because it needs (7,2,3); P1 cannot finish because it needs (0,2,0) after its earlier grant? It needs two units of B while only one is available; P2 needs six units of A; P3 needs one unit of B and one of C; P4 needs four units of A. No process can complete. Therefore the tentative request leads to an unsafe state and is rolled back.
6B. Compare deadlock strategies
Strategy	How it works	Advantages	Limitations for the cluster
Prevention	Break at least one necessary condition, e.g. impose a global resource order.	Can guarantee deadlock cannot occur under the enforced rule.	May reduce utilisation and flexibility; difficult if jobs need resources dynamically.
Avoidance	Grant a request only if the resulting state is safe (Banker’s algorithm).	Avoids entering unsafe states; useful when maximum needs are known.	Requires maximum demands in advance and safety check on requests.
Detection and recovery	Allow deadlocks, periodically detect cycles/unsafe waits, then abort or roll back jobs.	Does not require advance maximum claims; flexible allocation.	Detection costs CPU; aborted simulations lose completed work.
Ignore (ostrich)	Do nothing because deadlocks are considered sufficiently rare.	Very low operating overhead.	Unacceptable for critical shared laboratory resources if a cluster-wide stall is costly.

6C. Banker’s algorithm request steps
1.	Check that Request[i] is not greater than Need[i].
2.	Check that Request[i] is not greater than Available. If not, the process must wait.
3.	Temporarily allocate the requested resources and update Available, Allocation and Need.
4.	Run the safety algorithm. If a safe sequence exists, keep the allocation; otherwise restore the old state and deny the request.
6D. Predict complexity
Let n be the number of processes and m the number of resource types. In the straightforward safety algorithm, at most n processes are selected. Each search may scan up to n processes and compare up to m resource values. The conservative worst-case time complexity is O(m × n²). The work and finish arrays require O(n + m) extra space, excluding the input matrices.
