6F. Final design recommendation
For a research cluster where maximum resource requirements can be declared reliably, Banker’s algorithm is a strong choice for scarce, high-value resources because it prevents unsafe allocations before a deadlock occurs. The resource manager should collect maximum GPU, licence and storage requirements at job submission, run the safety check before granting requests, and provide queueing/fairness controls.
If jobs cannot predict their maximum requirements, use a hybrid design: enforce a resource ordering for common resources, use timeouts and deadlock detection for dynamic allocations, and recover by checkpointing or aborting the lowest-cost job. The final decision should consider the cost of a safety check, the cost of lost simulation work and whether maximum claims are trustworthy.
Final Conclusion
Process and thread design, suitable scheduling, reliable IPC, correct synchronisation and deadlock handling all contribute to better CPU utilisation and system performance. A bounded worker pool controls overload; MLFQ balances short and long jobs; shared memory is suitable for high-volume exchange when synchronisation is correct; fine-grained locks improve concurrency; and Banker’s algorithm avoids unsafe resource allocation when maximum demands are known.
